          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does adherence mean in the context of diabetes treatment, and why does it matter?",
          "answer": "In the context of diabetes treatment, \"adherence\" refers to how consistently a person follows their recommended treatment plan. This is particularly important for diabetes because it is largely a self-managed condition, meaning many decisions affecting the outcome are made by the individual between healthcare appointments, as described in the \"Sticking with a diabetes regimen\" document.\n\nAdherence matters significantly because poor adherence to glucose-lowering medication is common and can impact treatment effectiveness. Factors like cost, treatment complexity, side effects, low health literacy, depression, and simply forgetting can all contribute to challenges with adherence. Addressing these factors can improve adherence. Furthermore, structured diabetes self-management education is a core part of care that has been shown to improve glucose control and quality of life, according to the same source.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
